`,o.jsx(t.li,{children:"the exact first affected release has not been established."}),`
`]}),`
`,o.jsx(t.p,{children:"Sudo's own documentation states that regular-expression command specifications were introduced in 1.9.10. Releases before 1.9.10 are therefore outside this specific regex-command bug class. I have not established that every release from 1.9.10 through 1.9.17p2 contains the vulnerable fallback, so the final affected range should be based on upstream maintainer analysis and distribution backport history."}),`
`,o.jsx(t.h2,{id:"takeaway",children:"Takeaway"}),`
`,o.jsx(t.p,{children:"The security lesson is broader than sudo: canonicalisation cannot be used only for the access-control decision. Once a program decides that one filesystem object is trusted, execution must stay bound to that same object or trusted pathname."}),`
`,o.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Here, sudo correctly discovered that the submitted path resolved to ",o.jsx(t.code,{children:"/usr/bin/true"}),", but the vulnerable path lost that identity before execution. A single user-controlled symlink was enough to turn the gap into root code execution."]}),`
